[[File:Orange Clockvine Thunbergia gregorii.jpg|thumb|Orange clockvine, flowering in May, in [[Mountain View, California]]]]

'''''Thunbergia gregorii''''', commonly known as '''orange clockvine''' or '''orange trumpet vine''', is a herbaceous perennial climbing plant species in the family [[Acanthaceae]], native to East Africa and sometimes cultivated as an ornamental vine. The bright, pure all-orange flowers distinguish it from the related black-eyed Susan vine (''[[Thunbergia alata]]'').<ref name="Armitage">{{cite book|last=Armitage|first=Allan M. |title=Armitage's Vines and Climbers: A Gardener's Guide to the Best Vertical Plants|publisher=Timber Press|date=2011|pages=193|isbn=9781604692891|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=GBV-kXym4DkC&dq=Thunbergia+gregorii&pg=PA192}}</ref>

[[Spencer Le Marchant Moore]] described the species in 1894, naming it after [[John Walter Gregory]].<ref name="Armitage"/> Within the genus ''[[Thunbergia]]'', it is most closely related to ''T. alata'', the two being placed in the subgenus ''Parahexacentris''.<ref name="Schönenberger">{{cite journal|last=Schönenberger|first=Jürg|date=1999|title=Floral structure, development and diversity in ''Thunbergia'' (Acanthaceae)|journal=Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society|volume=130|issue=1|pages=1–36 [10–11]|doi=10.1111/j.1095-8339.1999.tb00779.x|doi-access=free}}</ref> The common name of clockvine relates to the vine spiralling upwards in a clockwise direction.<ref name="Armitage"/>

''Thunbergia gregorii'' is an evergreen vine that grows to 8–10&nbsp;ft. tall, or if left without support can become an extensive groundcover.  It looks like the related [[Thunbergia alata|black-eyed Susan vine]] (''T. alata''), but without the black eye.

''Thunbergia gregorii'' is native to east Africa, and is pollinated by bees.<ref name="Schönenberger"/>

Hardy to -1 C, ''Thunbergia gregorii'' flowers more profusely and even year-round in warmer climates, while restricted to summer and autumn in cooler climates.<ref>{{cite book|last=Mathias|first=Mildred E.|title=Flowering Plants in the Landscape|publisher=University of California Press|date=1985|pages=126|isbn=9780520054141|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=fxmT82tfAJ4C&dq=Thunbergia+gregorii&pg=PA126}}</ref> It has received the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref name = RHSPF>{{cite web | url = https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/101775/Thunbergia-gregorii/Details | title = ''Thunbergia gregorii'' | website = www.rhs.org | publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | access-date = 5 March 2021}}</ref>

Propagation is by cuttings taken in the summer.<ref name="Armitage"/>

